<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Führerprinzip</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leader principle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Führerprinzip, or leader principle, was the basis of authority in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It held that the leader's word stood above written law and that each official was responsible to his superior and owed him unconditional obedience, with all power flowing ultimately from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>. Applied to the party, the state and business, it replaced collective decision-making and elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip.jpg" alt="Führerprinzip" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nazi Party · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wochenspruch_der_NSDAP_16_February_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
